---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import ConceptMap from "../components/ConceptMap.astro";
import Doodle from "../components/Doodle.astro";
import Portrait from "../components/Portrait.astro";
import Says from "../components/Says.astro";
import FruitDemo from "../components/FruitDemo.astro";
import { CAST } from "../lib/cast";
import { withBase } from "../lib/base";

const chapters = (await getCollection("chapters", ({ data }) => !data.draft)).sort(
  (a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order,
);
const extras = await getCollection("extras", ({ data }) => !data.draft);
const first = chapters[0];
const total = chapters.reduce((n, c) => n + c.data.minutes, 0);

const PARTS = [
  { id: "orientation",   title: "Getting our bearings", art: "inversion",
    gist: "What this subject actually is, how it differs from ordinary programming, and a working model running in your browser before we explain a single thing about it." },
  { id: "foundations",   title: "How learning happens", art: "hill",
    gist: "How a program measures how wrong it is, and how it uses that to get less wrong. This part is small and it never changes — a language model learns the same way the fruit demo does." },
  { id: "deep-learning", title: "Neural networks",      art: "layers",
    gist: "What a neural network is made of, worked out on paper first, and then the same thing again in PyTorch in a tenth of the code." },
  { id: "frontier",      title: "The modern stack",     art: "beam",
    gist: "Pictures, words, and attention — and how a program that only ever guesses the next word ended up being able to answer questions." },
  { id: "practice",      title: "Doing it for real",    art: "ship",
    gist: "Reading a research paper without drowning, choosing what to measure, putting a model somewhere people can use it, and noticing when machine learning is the wrong tool." },
];
---

<Base title="Gradient">
  <main class="home" id="main">
    <div class="hero">
      <div>
        <p class="home__eyebrow">A slow, friendly walk through</p>
        <h1 class="home__title">how machines<br />learn things.</h1>
        <p class="home__lede">
          Start from nothing. You don't need to be a programmer, and the maths
          never goes past what school gave you. Nearly all the code on this site
          runs right here in the page — you press a button, and it goes.
        </p>
        <div class="home__cta">
          {first && <a class="btn btn--primary" href={withBase(`/learn/${first.id}/`)}>Start at the beginning →</a>}
          <a class="btn btn--ghost" href={withBase("/map/")}>See where it all goes</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="hero__art"><Doodle name="pipWave" /></div>
    </div>

    <h2>So what is machine learning?</h2>
    <p>
      Normally, if you want a computer to do something, you tell it exactly what
      to do. First this, then that, and if such-and-such then this other thing.
      Somebody works out every step and writes them all down, and the computer
      follows them without ever knowing what any of it is for. That's ordinary
      programming, and for anything you can spell out completely, it works
      beautifully.
    </p>
    <p>
      The catch is that most of what people know can't be spelled out. You can
      pick your friend out of a crowd in about a tenth of a second. Now try
      writing down how. Not <em>do it</em> — describe it. What exactly do you
      check, and in what order? You could sit there all week and not get it
      down, and you would still spot your friend instantly.
    </p>
    <p>
      Machine learning is what people do about that. Instead of writing the
      steps, you gather examples — a pile of photos, each one already labelled
      with whose face it is — and you hand them to a program whose job is to
      work out the steps itself. It doesn't come up with anything a person would
      recognise as instructions. It comes up with a long arithmetic recipe that
      happens to give the right answers, and it finds that recipe by starting
      with a hopeless one and nudging it towards better, over and over, a
      few thousand times.
    </p>
    <p>
      That's the idea. Everything on this site is either that idea in more
      detail, or the machinery that makes it work on something harder than
      fruit.
    </p>

    <Says who="wren">
      <p>
        Hello. Something to hang onto while you read: nobody ever tells these
        programs what a face <em>is</em>. They only ever see examples. Whatever
        the program ends up knowing, it worked out on its own, from pictures.
        That is still a strange thing to be true, and it's fine to keep finding
        it strange.
      </p>
    </Says>

    <h2>Here, have a go</h2>
    <p>
      Below is a board of fruit. Each dot is one piece of fruit that somebody
      weighed and tasted: further right means sweeter, further up means bigger.
      Red ones are apples, blue ones are blueberries.
    </p>
    <p>
      Nobody has told the computer anything about fruit. Press{" "}
      <strong>teach it</strong> and it will start with a line in a random,
      useless place and shuffle it around until the apples are on one side and
      the blueberries are on the other. Then switch to <strong>ask it</strong>,
      click somewhere empty, and it will tell you what it reckons goes there.
    </p>

    <FruitDemo />

    <p>
      That is a real machine learning model, not a cartoon of one. The same
      handful of ideas — a way of scoring how wrong you are, and a rule for
      getting less wrong — are what run underneath the systems that read your
      handwriting and finish your sentences. They're just doing it with rather
      more than two numbers per fruit.
    </p>

    <h2>Who's here</h2>
    <p>
      Four people turn up in the margins now and then. You'll get to know who's
      who by the face before you read the label.
    </p>
    <ul class="cast-strip">
      {CAST.map((m) => (
        <li class={`cast-card cast-card--${m.tone}`}>
          <Portrait who={m.id} class="cast-card__face" />
          <div>
            <p class="cast-card__name">{m.name}</p>
            <p class="cast-card__role">{m.role}</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <div class="pip-note">
      <Doodle name="pip" decorative />
      <p>
        And this is Pip, who is a ball. Pip's entire skill is rolling downhill,
        which sounds like a joke until chapter five, where it turns out to be
        how essentially all of this works.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Who this is for</h2>
    <p>
      Anyone who wants to know how this stuff works. There's no entrance exam.
      If you've never written a line of code, you're in the right place — the
      code is explained as it appears, and you can run all of it by pressing a
      button rather than installing anything.
    </p>
    <p>
      It was put together by someone who writes software for a living, and there
      are a few notes tucked away for people in the same boat — comparisons to
      languages you might already know, that sort of thing. Those are always
      folded shut and labelled, and nothing later depends on them. If they mean
      nothing to you, walk straight past.
    </p>

    <Says who="moss" flip>
      <p>
        One promise, from someone who has been lost in this material more than
        once: if a paragraph stops making sense, that is almost always the
        writing's fault, not yours. Where people reliably get stuck, there's a
        box waiting with the confusion written out and answered.
      </p>
    </Says>

    <h2>How the code works</h2>
    <p>
      When you press <strong>Run</strong> on a block of code, your browser
      quietly downloads a copy of Python and runs the code inside the page. The
      first time takes a few seconds; after that it's instant. Nothing gets
      installed, nothing is sent anywhere, and it all keeps working on a plane.
    </p>
    <p>
      Three of the later chapters need a heavier toolkit than a browser can
      hold. Those say so plainly, and there's a{" "}
      <a href={withBase("/setup/")}>setup guide</a> that walks through getting
      Python onto your own machine — on Windows, macOS or Linux — a step at a
      time, assuming nothing.
    </p>
    <p>
      Words from Python are underlined the first time they appear; hover or tap
      one and a short explanation comes up. Anything mathematical is folded
      away, so you can read the whole path without opening a single derivation,
      and every one of them is there the moment you're curious.
    </p>

    <h2>The path</h2>
    <div class="parts">
      {
        PARTS.map((part, i) => {
          const items = chapters.filter((c) => c.data.part === part.id);
          if (!items.length) return null;
          return (
            <div class={`part part--${i}`}>
              <div class="part__head">
                <span class="part__art"><Doodle name={part.art} decorative /></span>
                <h3>Part {["I", "II", "III", "IV", "V"][i]} · {part.title}</h3>
              </div>
              <p>{part.gist}</p>
              <ol>
                {items.map((c) => (
                  <li><a href={withBase(`/learn/${c.id}/`)}>{c.data.title}</a></li>
                ))}
              </ol>
            </div>
          );
        })
      }
    </div>
    <p class="home__meta">
      {chapters.length} chapters · roughly {Math.round(total / 60)} hours of
      reading and pressing buttons. Two weeks at an hour a day, or a month at
      half that. It isn't a race and nothing expires.
    </p>

    <h2>When you want more</h2>
    <p>
      Keeping to two weeks means leaving things out, and plenty of what got left
      out is worth reading. It lives in{" "}
      <a href={withBase("/extras/")}>{extras.length} extras</a> — the deeper
      whys, the roads not taken, and the things you only find out by trying.
      Nothing in the main path needs any of it.
    </p>

    <h2>Where it all fits together</h2>
    <p>
      Here's the shape of the subject on one screen. Solid boxes are taught
      here; dashed ones exist but are outside what we cover. Most of it won't
      mean anything yet — that's expected, and it's the reason the map is on the
      first page rather than the last. Come back after each part and watch it
      fill in.
    </p>
    <ConceptMap compact />
    <p class="home__meta">
      <a href={withBase("/map/")}>Open the map on its own page</a> for the full
      size, where every box explains itself when you hover over it.
    </p>
  </main>
</Base>
